|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_ABCD_roles_

    Let

    $$
    f(x) = A\cos(Bx + C) + D
    $$

    and imagine that each of the constants
    $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ is connected to a dial,
    and we originally set $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$
    to $1$, $1$, $0$ and $0$, respectively.
    Then, in the following order, we...

    |> List
        type=decimal
        style=padding-left:25%;

        ...increase $C$ from $0$ to $1{}.33$; and

        ...increase $B$ from $1$ to $2.33$; and
        !! ...increase $B$ from $1$ to $2.75$; and

        ...increase $A$ from $1$ to $3.33$; and
        !! ...decrease $A$ from $1$ to $-0.51$; and

        ...increase $D$ from $1$ to $4.33$.
        !! ...increase $D$ from $0$ to $3.99$.

    How does the graph of $f$ react to each
    of these changes, as they occur in sequence?

    !!   (x -> x + C) circ (x -> Bx)
    !! = (x -> Bx) circ (x -> x/B) circ (x -> x + C) circ (x -> Bx)
    !! = (x -> Bx) circ (x -> (Bx + C) / B)
    !! = (x -> Bx) circ (x -> x + C/B)

    !!   (u -> u + D) circ (u -> Au)
    !! = (u -> u + D) circ (u -> Au) circ (u -> u - D) circ (u -> u + D)
    !! = (u -> A(u - D) + D) circ (u -> u + D)
    !! = (u -> Au + D(1 - A)) circ (u -> u + D)

    |> Solution

        One can understand

        $$
        f = x \ra A\sin(Bx + C) + D
        $$

        as a 5-fold composition (with functions
        boxes going right to left):

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_general_comp.svg

        In the initial setting of the variables
        with $A = B = 1$ and $C = D = 0$
        the four boxes on the periphery are the
        identity*:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp.svg

        (*When we say “the identity” we mean 

        __the identity function__

        which is the function $x \ra x$.)

        As we dial $C$ up from $0$ to $1.33$,
        !! some nonzero value, 
        the box to the right of sin is
        “brought online”:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_C.svg

        The effect of adding such a box to the
        right of sin is to translate the
        graph of sin to the left by $C$, as discussed
        in Exercise >>_four_compositions_graphed_.
        The second step in which $B$ is increased from
        $1$ to $2.33$, brings the rightmost box online:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_B.svg

        Because this box is to the right of all
        we have so far (which is $x \ra \sin(x + C)$),
        it compresses the [current] graph horizontally by
        a factor $B$, also as discussed in Exercise >>_four_compositions_graphed_;
        step 3 brings the box immediately to the
        left of sin online...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_A.svg

        ...which scales the graph-so-far vertically by a
        factor $A$, as discussed in Exercise >>_four_compositions_graphed_,
        and finally step 4 brings the leftmost box online...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_D.svg

        ...that vertically translates the entire graph,
        as it stands, by $D$ units up, as again discussed
        in Exercise >>_four_compositions_graphed_.

        To recapitulate, starting from $y = \sin(x)$...

        |> List
            style=padding-left:33px;padding-right:2em;

            step 1: translates the graph left by $1.33\,\,(= C)$
            
            step 2: compresses the resulting graph horizontally by $2.33\,\,(= B)$ about the $y$ axis 

            step 3: dilates the resulting graph vertically by $3.33\,\,(= A)$ about the $x$ axis 

            step 4: translates the resulting graph up by $4.33\,\,(= D)$

        ...and that's the end.

        |> SolutionNote

            For simplicity of analysis it's important
            to bring the pre- and post-processing
            functions online “from the inside out”,
            gluing new boxes on to what's already there:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_inside_out.svg

            Otherwise, to give an example of what may
            go wrong, say that we first bring the rightmost
            box online:

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_rightmost.svg

            At this stage we have

            $$
            x \ra \sin(Bx)
            $$

            which is a copy of $\sin(x)$ horizontally
            compressed by $B = 2.33$. Then if we bring
            $x \ra x + C$ online...

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_middle.svg

            ...at this stage $x \ra x + C$ is
            !! neither a pre-processing nor a post-processing function; 
            NEITHER A PRE-PROCESSING NOR A POST-PROCESSING FUNCTION; 
            the effect of adding this function is
            NOT a translation of the graph by $C$ units in any
            direction!; instead, the effect of inserting this
            intermediate function into the chain is
            that the graph is translated left by 

            $$
            C/B
            $$

            units, as we can verify by rewriting

            $$
            x \ra \sin(Bx + C)
            $$

            (the function above), as

            $$
            x \ra \sin(B(x + C/B))
            $$

            ...which corresponds to this modified assemblage
            of boxes...

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_rearranged.svg

            ...and in which you can see that, after we
            dial up $B$ (introducing the “$x \ra Bx$” box),
            the effect of dialing up $C$ is to translate
            the graph left by $C/B$ units. 
            (The point is, if you

            __translate-and-then-compress__

            or if you
            
            __compress-and-then-translate__

            the two amounts of translation required are
            not the same, to achieve the same end result—if
            the translation comes second, it needs to
            be compressed, because everything else has
            already been compressed!) (And by the 
            way things blow up even worse if you attempt
            to dial $D$ up before dialing $A$ up, because if
            you attempt the same distributivity trick on the
            left-hand side to invert the order of the two boxes
            there...

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_identity_comp_rearranged_zz.svg

            |> ImageLeft
                src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_Au_cloud.svg

            ...you find that after $D$ has been dialed up,
            and it comes time to change $A$, there are TWO
            boxes left that depend on $A$!—so long story
            short, it becomes very difficult/annoying to
            analyze the effect that changing $A$ has on the
            graph, after $D$ is nonzero.)

        |> SolutionNote

            The problem uses ‘sin’ as an example but the
            same reasoning (with boxes) applies to any
            function. Namely, the graph

            $$
            y = A\cdot f(Bx + C) + D
            $$

            is the graph $y = f(x)$ translates left by
            $C$, compressed (horizontally) by $B$,
            scaled (vertically) by $A$, etc. (Well, “etc.”:
            translated vertically by $D$.)

            !! |> Image
            !!     src=images/e5_ABCD_roles_mona_lisa_cloud.svg
